nodecode-govee is a rewrite of promptclickrun's promptclickrun/hermes-govee:

  https://github.com/promptclickrun/hermes-govee
  commit 2d552cd1c67421cd5c9bbd8dd9b267f6dd5f0a0a
  Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2026 promptclickrun (LICENSE)

The plugin was one Hermes tool, govee_lights, and one skill that told the
model how to use it, over Govee's developer API. This rewrite keeps that
tool's name, its eight actions, its argument names, the four endpoints it
calls, and the rules it enforced: a light is found by exact device ID or by a
name that matches one light; a capability must be advertised before it is
used; brightness, colour and white temperature must sit inside the range the
device itself advertises; a scene name must match one scene; and after every
command the state is read back twice, about two seconds apart, with
"accepted" and "verified" reported apart so a lagging cloud is never mistaken
for a change. The plugin's skill is the cell's manual topic, (help :govee),
kept in its own voice: change only the lights asked for, never guess a target
from part of a name, never claim a change the read-back did not confirm, and
stop on a rate limit rather than retrying. The cell posts to Govee with
nlk:http, so no Python, no dependency and no local server are needed.
What was left out: Hermes's own secret lookup and secure-entry prompt (a
cell's section is where its key lives, so this one reads the section, then
GOVEE_API_KEY, and refuses at start when neither is set), the plugin
manifest, and its Python test suite, replaced by the offline tests here. Tool
refusals are marked as failed results, which is how Nodecode draws a failed
call, instead of riding back as an "ok": false object. The answer's members
are the original's own. Nothing of the original is vendored.
